#!/usr/bin/env python3
# Claude Code PreToolUse hook for the Bash tool. Four jobs:
#
#   1. Denies `git commit` when the command's EFFECTIVE target repo is on
#      master/main.
#   2. Reports core.hooksPath drift, and refuses the git subcommands the dead
#      hooks would have gated. See hooks_drift() for why this detector lives
#      here rather than in a git hook.
#   3. Denies `bd close` when its close reason cites a commit sha that no
#      remote-tracking ref in the effective repo contains (bd gqlc-90vt).
#   4. Denies the `bd` command that would mint a label GitHub cannot hold
#      (bd gqlc-uy7j).
#
# On (4): GitHub caps a label name at 50 characters and refuses the WHOLE issue
# over one bad label, so a bead OFFERED with one ends up with no mirror. bd
# accepts such a label silently at `bd create`, and the complaint is raised much
# later by .githooks/bd-gh-sync, at `git push`. Those are not the same session: measured
# 2026-08-24, gqlc-pyc6 carried a 79-character subject: label and EVERY push in
# town printed the refusal, while its author — the one person who knew what the
# label was for — never saw it and no reader had standing to edit someone else's
# bead. A red line that appears on every push and cannot be acted on by the
# person reading it is how a real failure becomes wallpaper.
#
# So this arm does not add a check; it moves an existing one to the boundary
# where the author is standing. The cap, and the remedy sentence it prints, are
# imported from .github/scripts/check-label-lengths.py rather than spelled again
# here — see label_rule(). Three gates now read that one file, so wherever a
# citizen meets this cap they meet the same words: always at the keyboard and in
# CI, and at the push for a bead that is not yet mirrored.
#
# The paragraph above is the CREATE path. A label added to a bead that ALREADY
# has a mirror never reaches GitHub at all — bd-gh-sync's screen skips any bead
# carrying an external_ref, and no pass in it offers a mirrored bead's labels
# again — so it draws no 422, no push-time NOTE, and CI is the first and only
# thing that objects. This arm covers both arrivals, which is why it keys on the
# subcommand rather than on `bd create` alone.
#
# What it does NOT reach, because a command line is all it sees: labels
# inherited from `--parent`, and labels written in a file by `bd create --file`
# or `--graph`. Both still meet bd-gh-sync at push time and the CI gate at PR
# time, which is why those two stay.
#
# On (3): the actor in the witnessed incident was not a git hook and not a bd
# feature — it was an agent running `bd close <id> -r "Closed by branch <b> at
# <sha> (N commits, not yet pushed)"` from this very tool. `.githooks/` carries
# no `bd close` call and `strings $(which bd)` carries no such template. This
# PreToolUse hook is the only thing in the repo that sees that command before it
# runs, which is why the guard lives here rather than in a git hook.
#
# The damage it prevents: a sha that exists only in one checkout is exactly what
# a rebase discards. The close survives the commit, so the ledger records work
# that never landed, and correcting it afterwards costs a bdguard exemption
# (bd gqlc-npyp). Refusing beats warning because a close that already happened
# IS the damage.
#
# Remote reachability is a PROXY for "this work exists somewhere other than one
# developer's index", not proof of it. What it does not establish, measured:
#   - a remote ref can be deleted later, so a sha reachable at close time can
#     become unreachable afterwards. gqlc squash-merges and deletes the branch,
#     so a close citing the BRANCH's own sha is orphaned once the PR lands; a
#     close citing the squashed commit on master is not. The guard reads the
#     repo at the moment of the close and says nothing about later — which is
#     why bdguard treats the same reachability as a veto rather than as
#     authorisation when it re-reads these closes in CI (bd gqlc-npyp).
#   - a remote can be a second local path (`git remote add x ../y`); "pushed"
#     there is not "pushed to origin". Ref set, not remote identity, is checked.
#   - remote TAGS and other refs/remotes-adjacent namespaces are not consulted:
#     the ref set is exactly `git branch -r --contains <sha>`, the command the
#     bead named, and the deny message names it so the reader can re-run it.
#
# Reach and refusal rate, replayed over every historical close reason in
# .beads/issues.jsonl at 15091c86 against this repo — 309 of them, of which the
# guard REFUSES 64, one close in five:
#
#   allow-no-sha            202      deny-unpushed-sha        51
#   allow-reachable          43      deny-unreadable-reason    7
#                                    deny-absent-object        6
#
# Most of the 51 are the guard working: `git merge-base --is-ancestor` agrees no
# remote ref holds those commits. The rate is the headline behavioural fact
# about this hook, and it is a snapshot — the corpus grows, and a sha reachable
# today can be orphaned by a squash-merge tomorrow. That replay also predates
# the all-decimal demotion at CITED_SHA_RE: 3 of the same 309 reasons carry an
# all-decimal ANCHORED token, so at most 3 rows move, and only out of the
# refused column.
#
# The target is resolved by analysis, not raw pattern-matching:
#   - a heredoc body whose delimiter HEREDOC_RE recognises is removed before
#     the text is tokenized (the forms it does not recognise are named in the
#     biases below), so a `git commit` written as prose in one is data rather
#     than a command. What a substitution INSIDE the body does is the next
#     sentence, and it does reach the tokenizer, one recursion down. The two
#     delimiter forms part company here, because the shell treats them
#     differently: a QUOTED
#     delimiter (<<'EOF', <<"EOF") expands nothing, so that body is dropped
#     whole; an UNQUOTED one (<<EOF) expands $(...) and `...` in its body, so
#     the unquoted body's substitutions are extracted and analyzed while its
#     prose still is not. What gets extracted is not the same set as what the
#     shell would run: the extractors key on a literal $( or ` without looking
#     at what precedes it, and the shell does look. Both directions of that
#     gap are disclosed below — the backslash escape under the biases, the
#     spellings the regexes cannot reach at all under the limitations.
#     Measured with a touch-marker fixture: the substitution under <<EOF
#     created the file, the identical one under <<'EOF' did not (bd gqlc-wa8c,
#     which was the missing half — the unquoted body used to be stripped
#     before substitutions were extracted, so a $(git commit) there was
#     silently allowed)
#   - $(...) and `...` substitution bodies are analyzed one recursion down,
#     including inside double quotes, where shlex hands back one opaque token
#     and the extractor is the only reader. The $( ) scan counts paren depth,
#     so a body holding a balanced pair is read: `-r "at <sha> (12 commits,
#     not yet pushed)"` is the witnessed incident's own reason text and the
#     majority spelling here — 192 of the 309 close reasons in
#     .beads/issues.jsonl at 15091c86 carry a paren, 106 of those a sha too.
#     Quoting is not tracked inside the scan, so a paren the shell would read
#     as a literal is counted, and the two unbalanced directions part company.
#     A surplus `(` never closes, so the span goes unread -> allow (pinned by
#     "a surplus ( leaves the span unclosed and unread"). A surplus `)` closes
#     the span early, so the body does come back, truncated there, and is
#     analyzed (pinned by "a surplus ) closes the span early"). The one
#     unbalanced reason among those 192 is the second direction, not the
#     first: gqlc-oxne, 6 `(` to 7 `)`, going negative 857 characters in on a
#     `case " ${a} ${b} " in *" ${x} "*)` pattern
#   - a heredoc opened INSIDE a `$( )`, including one written inside double
#     quotes, is a heredoc: the shell parses a command substitution as its own
#     command list, so `--body "$(cat <<'BODY' ... BODY)"` — how a PR body is
#     written here — arms and strips like any other. Reading that `<<` as
#     quoted prose is what refused two agents a `gh pr create` whose BODY
#     quoted the words `git commit` (bd gqlc-gtxl). Backticks inside double
#     quotes are NOT given the same reading, so a heredoc opened in one arms
#     nothing and its body reaches the tokenizer — the fail-closed side
#   - the rest is shlex-tokenized, so a multi-word quoted run collapses to one
#     token and stops matching: `echo "git commit -m x"` is silent (measured).
#     Word-by-word quoting does not collapse, so `echo "git" "commit" -m x`
#     still denies (measured, control `echo git commit` denies too). The error
#     is fail-closed, so it is left as is rather than widened into a parser
#   - `cd` chains and `git -C` options fold into an effective directory,
#     whose branch is read via git rev-parse / symbolic-ref (the latter
#     covers unborn HEADs). A substitution body folds the cd chain that
#     PRECEDES its span in its own scope, so `cd <worktree> && echo
#     "$(git commit)"` is read against the worktree rather than against the
#     directory this hook was invoked from. It used to be read against the
#     latter, which in this repo's workflow is the shared checkout parked on
#     master — a verdict about the wrong repository in BOTH directions, and
#     the false-negative one was measured too: `cd <master repo> && echo
#     "$(git commit)"` run from a feature checkout was allowed
#   - a redirection is not a terminator. The shell accepts one anywhere in a
#     simple command, so both option walks skip the operator and its target
#     and carry on; `;`, `&&`, `|` and `)` still end the command
#
# Accepted limitations, fail-open unless the entry says otherwise (server-side
# branch protection and repo pre-commit hooks are the backstop):
#   - paths that need expansion (cd "$WT", git -C ~/x) are unresolvable
#     statically -> allow
#   - cd/pushd with flags (cd -P x) or no argument lose tracking -> allow
#   - nested interpreters (bash -c '...') are not recursed into
#   - a substitution's cwd is read from the text before its span, which is
#     normally an unterminated fragment (`--body "`). One closing quote of each
#     kind is tried and then the enclosing directory is kept, which is the
#     pre-existing answer and errs toward naming a directory on master, i.e.
#     toward refusing. `cd "$WT"` stays unresolvable there as everywhere else
#   - --git-dir / --work-tree redirection is not resolved -> allow
#   - inside an unquoted heredoc body this hook has actually stripped, the
#     substitution extractor is the only thing looking, because the prose
#     around it has to stay data. A command it does not match is therefore not
#     seen there at all. The instances measured on bash 5.3.15, which is what
#     has been looked for rather than a proof that nothing else qualifies:
#     bash 5.3's ${ cmd; } funsub, which the extractor does not match; and a
#     backslash newline splitting the $( token itself, which leaves it nothing
#     to match while the shell rejoins the line and runs it — that one wrote a
#     real commit onto master in the fixture. Both DENY when written bare, the
#     tokenizer having split them into words there; both are silent inside a
#     stripped heredoc body -> allow. Both are silent inside DOUBLE QUOTES at
#     top level with no heredoc anywhere as well (rows `funsub ${ ; }
#     double-quoted` and `cont-split $( double-quoted`), shlex collapsing the
#     whole run to one token — so what saves the bare spelling is the absence
#     of quoting rather than the absence of a heredoc
#   - a `case` pattern's `)` inside a `$( )` closes the span early, because the
#     paren scan does not know shell grammar. What comes back is the body up to
#     that pattern, and the commands after it are left in the surrounding text —
#     where, double-quoted, shlex collapses them into one token and stops
#     matching. So `echo "$(case x in a) git commit -m y;; esac)"` is allowed
#     (measured, on this hook and on its parent alike), while the same body
#     without the case is denied. Pinned by `case pattern ) hides a later
#     commit`. This is the surplus-`)` direction below, reached by a spelling
#     whose parens balance in the shell's reading but not in this one
#   - HEREDOC_RE reads a \w+ delimiter, so <<EOF.txt and <<EOF-1 arm on the
#     prefix `EOF`. The real terminator line does not equal that, so the body
#     goes on swallowing until some line is exactly `EOF` — and when none is,
#     a real `git commit` written after the heredoc is never seen (measured)
#     -> allow. When one IS (a bare `EOF` inside the body), the swallow ends
#     early and the rest is tokenized, which denies (also measured), so the
#     failure direction here depends on the body's contents rather than on the
#     delimiter alone. Disclosed rather than widened: re-deriving the shell's
#     delimiter-word grammar is the parser this file declines to become, and
#     each widening moves rows nobody has measured
# Deliberate conservative biases:
#   - substitution extraction is textual, so $(...) inside single quotes
#     (which would not execute) is still analyzed
#   - the same textual extraction does not count the backslashes in front of a
#     $( or `, and the shell does. Inside an unquoted heredoc body the count is
#     what decides: across 0 to 4 measured, an odd count suppressed the
#     expansion and an even count did not, so \$(git commit) is inert while
#     \\$(git commit) runs. Neither parity is visible to the scan, so both are
#     extracted and both deny — including the escaped spelling, which is the
#     one you would write to DOCUMENT this guard in a README heredoc. Measured
#     with a positive-content witness rather than an absent marker: on the odd
#     rows the heredoc's target file still holds the literal $(...) text
#   - the token scan is position-agnostic, so UNQUOTED prose like
#     `echo git commit` still denies (quote it and it will not)
#   - the same \w+ bound cuts the other way for delimiter forms the regex
#     cannot match at all — <<\EOF, <<"EO F", <<'EOF.txt' — which arm nothing,
#     so their bodies are not stripped and do reach the tokenizer: prose there
#     denies, though all three are quoted forms that expand nothing (measured
#     for each of the three)
# If shlex cannot tokenize at all, falls back to the old whole-text regex
# checked at the hook's own cwd.
#
# What the hooksPath half does NOT catch. Fail-open unless the entry says
# otherwise (this runs before every Bash call in every concurrent session, so a
# false positive costs more than a missed detection):
#   - a repo whose .githooks/ directory is gone entirely: nothing then
#     distinguishes it from an unrelated repo that never had hooks, so the
#     check stays silent rather than firing in every repo on the machine
#   - the shlex fallback regex covers `git commit` only, so a command that
#     fails to tokenize and then merges, pulls or pushes is warned about but
#     not refused
#   - the config value is compared literally, not resolved: an absolute path
#     naming the very same directory still reads as drift (see HOOKS_DIRNAME)
#     and the gated subcommands are refused although the hooks it points at are
#     live. Fail-CLOSED, so this is an entry the heading's "unless" covers
#     (measured, against a control with the relative spelling that stays silent
#     in the same repo)
#   - an internal error in this hook warns and allows rather than denying (see
#     __main__): a bug here must not be able to block its own repair. It is no
#     longer *silent*, which is the part that mattered — a detector that exits
#     0 on its own bug is the failure this hook exists to catch.
#     The `bd close` arm is the exception: an exception raised INSIDE it is
#     caught there and turned into a refusal, because that arm's whole point is
#     that a close it could not check must not proceed (see main()).
#   - core.hooksPath disabled INLINE by the command under inspection, as in
#     `git -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null commit`: the env filter in hooks_drift()
#     reads what this process INHERITED, and an inline -c is not set anywhere
#     until git itself parses it. Measured allowed, 2026-08-22. This is the
#     same class as `git commit --no-verify`, which is also allowed here and
#     which the Constitution governs by rule rather than by hook (bd gqlc-0tsh).
#   - a working tree PARKED on a commit that predates any of the above. This is
#     the whole of bd gqlc-pyk2, and it is not fixable from inside this file or
#     from .claude/settings.json, which is what that bead proposed. The bead
#     reads the defect as a PATH problem — settings.json wires the hook
#     relatively, so it resolves against the working tree — and offers "an
#     absolute or git-common-dir-anchored path in settings.json" as the repair.
#     That is falsified by the carrier: .claude/settings.json is itself a
#     TRACKED file, so a checkout parked before the fix reads its own copy of
#     settings.json and never learns the new path. Every tracked artifact has
#     this shape; the escape is an untracked carrier (.claude/settings.local.json,
#     ~/.claude/settings.json) or the git common dir, and only the last of those
#     can be shipped by a commit — which is what .githooks/hooks-drift-tripwire
#     does for the git-hook channel (PR #1044). What actually bounds this today
#     is CLAUDE.md's rule that the shared checkout stays on master and every
#     session cuts a sibling worktree from origin/master. That is a discipline,
#     not a gate, and it is stated here rather than claimed closed.
#
# The `bd close` arm reads the reason as it is written on the command line, or
# out of a literal `--reason-file` path. Reaches, and does not reach:
#   - `-r`/`--reason`/`--reason-file` with a literal value -> READ. The
#     spellings pinned are two tokens, `--reason=VALUE`, `-rVALUE`, `-fr VALUE`
#     and `-qr=VALUE`; the cluster ones are ordinary usage here because `-f` is
#     how a pinned bead is force-closed, and each of them verdicted
#     `allow-no-reason` before scan_bd walked the cluster — a false positive
#     rather than a miss, since that verdict asserts the close records no sha.
#   - a reason token holding a `$` or a BACKTICK -> NOT readable, and DENIED
#     rather than skipped: the hook cannot tell such a reason cites nothing
#     from cites-something. Quoting is already resolved by the time the token
#     reaches here, so `-r "$SHA"` and `-r 'the $SHA literal'` are the same
#     token and both are refused; so is any reason carrying a markdown
#     backtick, which this repo writes routinely. Measured over the 309
#     historical close reasons: 7 hit this, 4 of them by a backtick alone.
#     `--reason-file -` is refused for the same reason, one layer up.
#   - a close with no reason flag at all -> allowed. It records no sha, so it
#     is outside this defect class. Stated rather than implied: the verdict is
#     `allow-no-reason`, a named branch, not a fall-through.
#   - `bd update <id> -s closed` -> NOT reached. `bd update` has no reason flag
#     (checked against `bd update --help`, v1.0.4), so it cannot cite a sha in
#     a close reason; a sha in its `--notes` is a different record and a
#     different defect.
#   - a close the tokenizer never sees -> NOT reached, fail-open. `sh -c '...'`,
#     `bash -lc "..."` and `eval "..."` are nested interpreters this hook does
#     not recurse into, and a command shlex cannot tokenize at all falls back to
#     FALLBACK_RE, which matches `git commit` and nothing else. A `bd close`
#     inside any of those runs unchecked. Left as is: the close arm's fallback
#     would have to re-derive the reason text from raw bytes, which is the
#     parser this file declines to become.
#
# Hook contract: JSON on stdin { "tool_name": "Bash",
#   "tool_input": { "command": "..." } }. Deny = exit 0 + JSON with
# permissionDecision "deny"; warn = exit 0 + JSON with systemMessage and
# additionalContext but NO permissionDecision (an explicit "allow" would
# waive the user's permission prompt); allow = exit 0, no output.
#
# Tests: .githooks/tests/claude-pre-bash-test.sh (run via `just test-hooks`).

import functools
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys

PROTECTED = ("master", "main")
# The value `just init` writes and `just check-hooks` requires. Relative on
# purpose: git resolves it against each working tree's root, so one shared
# .git/config activates the hooks OF the branch each linked worktree has
# checked out. Measured with a two-worktree fixture, the same commit in the
# linked worktree ran the linked worktree's pre-commit under `.githooks` and
# the MAIN worktree's under `/abs/path/to/main/.githooks` — so an absolute
# path is drift even when it names a directory full of live hooks.
HOOKS_DIRNAME = ".githooks"
# git subcommands refused while the hooks are dead, picked by measuring which
# ones run a hook this repo ships rather than by reasoning about it. Measured on
# git 2.55.0 against .githooks/{pre-commit,commit-msg,pre-push,post-merge}, each
# armed to log and exit 0 so the chain continues: `commit` and `commit --amend`
# fire pre-commit and commit-msg; a non-fast-forward `merge` or `pull` fires
# commit-msg and post-merge (not pre-commit); `push` fires pre-push. In the
# same fixture `revert`, `cherry-pick` (with and without -e), `rebase` and `am`
# fired none of the four. merge/pull are refused rather than warned about
# because post-merge runs bd-gh-sync. It used to be the ONLY thing a drifted
# merge lost: commit-msg fires on a merge, but its MERGE_HEAD guard exited 0
# ahead of both the identity checks and the Co-Authored-By scan, so a merge
# commit carrying a Claude trailer was accepted rc=0 with the hooks HEALTHY
# (measured, against a plain-commit control rejected rc=1 in the same repo).
# That was bd gqlc-7y7e and it is now fixed: the guard is split, the trailer
# scan runs on every message and only the identity arms stand down during a
# merge. A drifted merge or pull therefore loses the AI-attribution gate as well
# as the bd mirror, which strengthens the case for refusing it.
#
# Cited by construct (MERGE_HEAD), not by line number: the line range this
# comment used to name for that guard went stale the first time a line was
# inserted above it, and no suite noticed (bd gqlc-m83s). A row in
# .githooks/tests/commit-msg-test.sh now refuses such a citation anywhere in the
# tree.
# What a merge or pull fires varies by shape, and the subcommand this hook keys
# on does not determine the shape. Measured in the same fixture: a fast-forward
# `merge` or `pull` writes no commit but still fires post-merge, the bd-gh-sync
# mirror; `merge --squash` writes no commit either and also fires post-merge;
# `pull --rebase` fires post-merge while the branch is still fast-forwardable
# and nothing once it has diverged; plain `rebase` and a 3-way
# `merge --no-commit` fire nothing. `pull` alone therefore spans shapes firing
# two of the four, one, and none. Gating the subcommand refuses that last group
# too — the conservative side of a distinction this hook cannot make before the
# fact.
HOOK_GATED = ("commit", "merge", "pull", "push")
HEREDOC_RE = re.compile(r"(?<!<)<<(-?)\s*([\"']?)(\w+)\2")
FALLBACK_RE = re.compile(r"(?:^|[^A-Za-z-])git\s+commit(?:$|\s)")
OPERATOR_CHARS = set("();<>|&")
GIT_OPTS_WITH_ARG = ("-c", "--namespace", "--exec-path", "--super-prefix")
# The shell accepts a redirection ANYWHERE in a simple command, including
# between the subcommand and its flags, so a redirect does not end the word
# list the way `;`, `&&`, `|` and `)` do. shlex hands the operator back as its
# own token and splits an fd prefix off it (`2>&1` tokenizes as `2`, `>&`, `1`),
# so an option walk that stops at every operator token loses every flag written
# after the redirect. Both walks in this file did, and both directions were
# measured before the fix (bd gqlc-vmlp):
#   bd close gqlc-x > /dev/null -r "Closed at <orphan sha>."  -> allow-no-reason
#   git >/dev/null commit -m x                                -> ALLOWED on master
# i.e. it was a bypass of the master guard, not only of the close guard. Listed
# rather than derived from the operator characters, because `&&`, `|` and `;`
# share those characters and DO terminate the command.
REDIRECT_OPS = ("<", ">", ">>", ">&", "<&", "&>", "&>>", ">|", "<>")

# `bd close` arm. BD_OPTS_WITH_ARG exists so a flag VALUE spelled `close` is not
# read as the subcommand; a LONG flag outside that list is treated as boolean,
# which can only over-detect a close, and over-detection is inert (a close with
# no reason flag is allowed). Absence from the list does not make a shorthand
# boolean: a cluster is walked letter by letter (shorthand_arg), so unlisted
# `-fr` still consumes a value, which is what pflag does. The listed shorthands
# `-r` and `-C` are governed by it, bare and inline both — scan_bd tests the
# list first and only then falls through to shorthand_arg. `done` is bd's
# documented alias for `close`.
BD_CLOSE_SUBCOMMANDS = ("close", "done")
BD_OPTS_WITH_ARG = (
    "-C", "--directory", "--db", "--actor", "--dolt-auto-commit",
    "--session", "-r", "--reason", "--reason-file",
    "-l", "--labels", "--label", "--add-label", "--set-labels", "--remove-label",
)
# `bd label` arm (bd gqlc-uy7j). Which flags WRITE a label, per subcommand —
# and the per-subcommand table is the whole point, because `-l` is `--labels`
# on `create` and `--label`, a FILTER, on `list` and `ready`. One letter, two
# opposite meanings: a guard keyed on the flag alone would refuse a read.
# Measured against the bd on this host (`bd create --help`, `bd update --help`,
# `bd list --help`).
BD_LABEL_WRITE_OPTS = {
    "create": ("-l", "--labels"),
    "new": ("-l", "--labels"),          # bd's documented alias for create
    "update": ("--add-label", "--set-labels"),
}
# `--remove-label` is deliberately absent: it cannot mint a label, so screening
# it would only refuse the command that CLEARS an over-cap one.
#
# `bd label add [issue-id...] [label]` takes its label POSITIONALLY, with no
# flag to key on at all — it was the path still open when this arm screened
# flags only, and it writes for real (measured: `✓ Added label ... to probe-r07`
# in a throwaway workspace). The label is the last positional. `bd label remove`
# is the inverse and is not screened; `bd label propagate` copies a label that
# already exists, so it can mint nothing new.
BD_LABEL_POSITIONAL = ("label", "add")
# bd is cobra/pflag, which lets a shorthand carry its value attached (-rTEXT)
# and lets shorthands cluster (-fr TEXT, -qr=TEXT), and this repo force-closes
# pinned beads with -f. Measured against bd v1.0.4: `-rZZZ` and `-fr ZZZ` both
# parse, `-fr` with nothing after it answers "flag needs an argument: 'r'", and
# `-Z` answers "unknown shorthand flag". Of these, only `r` and `C` take a value
# in `bd close --help`; -f, -q, -v and -h are boolean, so a cluster is walked
# until a value-taking letter is reached.
#
# `l` is here for `bd create -l` and does NOT appear in `bd close --help` at all,
# so on a close it makes the scanner consume one token pflag would have rejected.
# That is the safe direction: over-consuming can only hide a token from the
# subcommand test, and a close the scanner fails to see is allowed, which is this
# guard's pre-existing bias rather than a new one. The mapping is per-shorthand
# and not per-subcommand because a cluster is walked before any subcommand is
# known; what the letter MEANS is decided later, by BD_LABEL_WRITE_OPTS.
BD_SHORTHANDS_WITH_ARG = {"r": "--reason", "C": "--directory", "l": "--labels"}
# Two tiers, because a sha is recognised by two different kinds of evidence.
#
# CITED_SHA_RE is the `at <sha>` / `@ <sha>` / `commit <sha>` idiom. A match is
# treated as a citation EVEN WHEN THE OBJECT IS ABSENT, which is the only thing
# that makes an invented or foreign sha refusable. It is not the majority shape:
# measured over .beads/issues.jsonl at 15091c86, 110 close reasons cite a hex
# token and this anchor reaches 31 of them (28%). `at|@` alone reached 26;
# adding `sha <x>` reached no further reason, so it is not here.
#
# SHA_RE is every other hex-looking token, and one of those is a citation only
# once git says it names a commit in this checkout. English words are all-hex
# often enough (`defaced`, `effaced`) that trusting an unresolvable loose token
# would refuse real closes.
#
# So the 79 unanchored reasons are reached only while their commit still exists
# locally. That is not a gap for the defect this guard exists for — an unpushed
# commit is by construction present in the object store of the checkout that
# just made it — but it IS a gap for a close citing a sha from somewhere else
# entirely: unanchored and unresolvable, that is allowed through.
#
# Both halves are case-insensitive, and each half was measured to matter alone.
#
# The hex class: git resolves uppercase hex, so `-r "Closed at 453E5D20."` named
# a real unpushed commit while `[0-9a-f]` saw no sha at all and the close was
# allowed. Neither tier reached it — the anchored one because the class did not
# match, the loose one for the same reason — so uppercase was a total bypass of
# a guard whose whole subject is the sha.
#
# The anchor words: `(?i:)` and not a lowercase list, because a reason opening a
# sentence with `Commit 4491fb8` is ordinary English and the anchor missed it on
# the capital alone. That one is not merely cosmetic — it is the documented gap
# two paragraphs up. Anchored citations are refused even when the object is
# absent, so a sentence-initial `Commit <foreign sha>` moves from allowed to
# refused. Measured over .beads/issues.jsonl at eb28226b: of 498 text fields,
# 4 gain an anchored citation and exactly 1 changes verdict — gqlc-v23, whose
# `Commits 4491fb8(model)` names no object in this checkout. That is the gap
# closing on a real instance, not a false positive.
#
# No corpus reason gains anything from the hex half; it is preventive. Widening
# it does put all-hex words in ALL CAPS (`ACCEDED`, `EFFACED`) within reach of
# the loose tier, which is harmless there — the loose tier promotes a token to a
# citation only once git resolves it — but within the anchored tier `at EFFACED`
# would now be refused on the word alone. No corpus reason has that shape.
#
# An ALL-DECIMAL token is demoted out of the anchored tier and left to the loose
# one (bd gqlc-7fn3). The anchored tier refuses without resolving anything, so
# `at 1048576` — a tmpfs inode budget quoted out of a citizen's own measurement —
# was refused three times in a row on a close that was otherwise correct, and the
# only remedy was to make the reason LESS precise. Byte counts, row totals and
# epoch timestamps are exactly what a close reason built from command output
# carries, so the guard fired hardest on the closes carrying the most evidence.
#
# Demotion, not exclusion: the loose tier promotes a token to a citation once
# git says it names a commit here, so an all-decimal abbreviated sha that really
# is an unpushed commit in this checkout is STILL refused — measured, and pinned
# by a row. What moves is only the token git cannot resolve: `at 1048576` in a
# repo with no such object is now allow-no-sha rather than deny-absent-object.
# The residue is a foreign all-decimal sha (a real commit elsewhere, absent
# here) written after an anchor word, which becomes allowed. About 3.7% of
# 7-character abbreviations are all-decimal ((10/16)^7), so excluding them from
# BOTH tiers would have been a real hole; excluding them from the tier that
# refuses on the anchor alone is not.
CITED_SHA_RE = re.compile(r"(?i:\bat\s+|@\s*|\bcommits?\s+)([0-9a-fA-F]{7,40})(?![0-9A-Za-z])")
SHA_RE = re.compile(r"(?<![0-9A-Za-z])([0-9a-fA-F]{7,40})(?![0-9A-Za-z])")
DECIMAL_TOKEN_RE = re.compile(r"\A[0-9]+\Z")
# Anything the shell would expand. `$5` is not in it on purpose: a bare digit is
# a positional parameter no reason ever means, and prices appear in prose.
UNEXPANDED_RE = re.compile(r"\$[({A-Za-z_]|`")


def is_op(tok):
    return bool(tok) and all(c in OPERATOR_CHARS for c in tok)


def redirect_skip(tokens, j):
    """Tokens a redirection at tokens[j] consumes, or 0 when it is not one.

    An optional fd prefix, the operator, and its target word. The fd prefix is
    a separate ORDINARY token to shlex — `2>&1` comes back as `2`, `>&`, `1` —
    so a walk that keys on operator tokens alone never sees the `2`, and the
    git walk read it as the subcommand and stopped: `git 2>&1 commit -m x` was
    allowed onto master (measured, bd gqlc-vmlp). A redirect whose next token
    is itself an operator — `cmd > ; x`, which the shell rejects — consumes the
    operator alone, so the terminator stays visible to the caller's walk rather
    than being swallowed as a filename.
    """
    k, n = j, len(tokens)
    if DECIMAL_TOKEN_RE.match(tokens[k]) and k + 1 < n and tokens[k + 1] in REDIRECT_OPS:
        k += 1
    if tokens[k] not in REDIRECT_OPS:
        return 0
    k += 1
    if k < n and not is_op(tokens[k]):
        k += 1
    return k - j


def unquoted_positions(line, state):
    # Bash quote parity, carried across lines (quoted strings span newlines).
    #
    # `state` is a list used as a stack: the innermost quoting context is at the
    # end and an empty stack means unquoted. A command substitution reopens an
    # UNQUOTED context even inside double quotes — the shell parses `$( ... )`
    # as its own command list, so `--body "$(cat <<'EOF' ... EOF)"` arms a real
    # heredoc that the surrounding double quotes have no say over. Tracking only
    # the quote character read that `<<` as prose, so the heredoc did not arm,
    # the body was not stripped, and a `git commit` QUOTED IN A PR BODY was
    # extracted from it and refused as a commit onto master — twice in one night,
    # to two different agents (bd gqlc-gtxl). Backticks are not given the same
    # treatment: inside double quotes their escaping rules differ from `$( )`'s
    # and no measured incident used that spelling, so a `<<` inside a
    # double-quoted backtick substitution still reads as quoted -> no heredoc
    # armed -> its body reaches the tokenizer, which is the fail-CLOSED side.
    outside = set()
    i, n = 0, len(line)
    while i < n:
        c = line[i]
        top = state[-1] if state else None
        if top is None or top == "(":
            if c == "\\":
                i += 2
                continue
            if c in "'\"":
                state.append(c)
            elif c == "$" and i + 1 < n and line[i + 1] == "(":
                outside.add(i)
                state.append("(")
                i += 2
                continue
            elif c == ")" and top == "(":
                state.pop()
            else:
                outside.add(i)
        elif top == '"':
            if c == "\\":
                i += 2
                continue
            if c == '"':
                state.pop()
            elif c == "$" and i + 1 < n and line[i + 1] == "(":
                state.append("(")
                i += 2
                continue
        elif c == "'":
            state.pop()
        i += 1
    return outside, state


def strip_heredocs(text):
    # -> (text with every heredoc body removed, bodies of the UNQUOTED ones)
    #
    # Both returns are needed because the two delimiter forms differ in the
    # shell, not just in this hook: a quoted delimiter (<<'EOF', <<"EOF")
    # expands nothing, so its body is inert and the second return drops it; an
    # unquoted one (<<EOF) expands $(...) and `...`, so the caller has to
    # analyze them. The second return is the body text as written: whether any
    # particular substitution in it would survive to run is not decided here,
    # and not by the caller's regexes either, which do not read the backslash
    # escapes the shell honours (see the header's biases).
    # Group 2 of HEREDOC_RE is
    # the quote character, empty when the matched delimiter carries none. The
    # forms the regex matches nothing for at all (<<\EOF and friends) arm no
    # heredoc here and are disclosed in the header instead.
    kept, pending, expanded = [], [], []
    qstate = []
    for line in text.split("\n"):
        if pending:
            delim, tab_strip, quoted = pending[0]
            if (line.lstrip("\t") if tab_strip else line) == delim:
                pending.pop(0)
            elif not quoted:
                expanded.append(line)
            continue
        # Only arm on << outside quotes: a quoted "<< X" is prose, and arming
        # on it would strip real commands from the following lines.
        outside, qstate = unquoted_positions(line, qstate)
        for m in HEREDOC_RE.finditer(line):
            if m.start() in outside:
                pending.append((m.group(3), m.group(1) == "-", bool(m.group(2))))
        kept.append(line)
    return "\n".join(kept), "\n".join(expanded)


def paren_matches(text):
    """Map from each `(` in text to the `)` that closes it, built in one pass.

    Parens are counted rather than excluded, which is what lets a substitution
    body hold one: `bd close x -r "at 1234567 (12 commits, not yet pushed)"` is
    the majority spelling of this repo's close reasons — 192 of the 309 in
    .beads/issues.jsonl at 15091c86 carry a paren and 106 of those carry a sha
    as well — and it is the witnessed incident's own reason text.

    One pass, not a depth count restarted at each `$(`: an unclosed `$(` has no
    closing paren to find, so a per-span count read to end-of-text before
    giving up, and a text holding many of them cost O(n^2). End-to-end hook
    wall time on a heredoc of lone `$(` lines, restarted count vs this one:
    14 KB 447 ms -> 23 ms, 57 KB 7071 ms -> 26 ms, 144 KB >= 30 s (killed at a
    30 s CPU cap) -> 42 ms. This hook runs before every Bash call. A length cap
    was rejected instead: text past the cap would go unanalyzed, which turns a
    slow path into a silent one.

    Deep NESTING used to be quadratic on top of this, because the extractor
    re-split each body it popped and each such call mapped that body again.
    substitution_bodies() now walks the text once with a frame stack and never
    re-reads a body, so this map is built exactly once per git_targets level.
    End-to-end hook wall time on a heredoc of `$(` x d then `)` x d, both
    versions run through the same harness on one machine (bd gqlc-hv81):

        d = 200    23 ms -> 20 ms
        d = 800    53 ms -> 23 ms
        d = 3200  497 ms -> 36 ms

    The extraction call alone, which is the part that changed, went 1.7/28/454
    ms -> 0.1/0.5/1.9 ms at those depths; the residue is interpreter start-up.

    Dropping a `)` that has nothing open is what makes this identical to the
    restarted count it replaces — verified exhaustively over every string of
    `(`, `)` and `x` up to length 12, and over 200k random strings of shell
    metacharacters.
    """
    matches, stack = {}, []
    for j, c in enumerate(text):
        if c == "(":
            stack.append(j)
        elif c == ")" and stack:
            matches[stack.pop()] = j
    return matches


def cd_target(text, cwd):
    """The directory `text`'s cd/pushd chain leaves the shell in.

    Used to give a substitution body the directory the shell would run it in,
    rather than the directory this hook happens to have been invoked from. That
    mattered because the mandated workflow here writes every command as
    `cd <sibling worktree> && ...` while the tool's own cwd is the shared
    checkout, which is parked on master: a body analyzed at the tool's cwd is a
    verdict about the wrong repository (bd gqlc-gtxl).

    Position is what makes this safe in the other direction. The caller passes
    the ENCLOSING SCOPE'S KEPT TEXT UP TO THE SPAN — text with earlier sibling
    spans already blanked, and nothing that follows the span — so a `cd` the
    shell has not run yet (`$(git commit) && cd /elsewhere`) and a `cd` that ran
    in an earlier subshell (`$(cd /elsewhere) && $(git commit)`) both leave this
    answer alone. Either would otherwise resolve a real master commit against
    some other repo and allow it.

    A prefix normally ends mid-quote, `--body "` being the shape that motivated
    this, so a failure to tokenize is expected rather than exceptional: one
    closing quote of each kind is tried, and if none of the three parses the
    enclosing cwd is kept. That is the pre-fix behaviour, which errs toward
    naming a directory on master, i.e. toward refusing.
    """
    if "cd" not in text and "pushd" not in text:
        return cwd
    tokens = None
    for candidate in (text, text + '"', text + "'"):
        try:
            lex = shlex.shlex(candidate, posix=True, punctuation_chars=True)
            lex.whitespace_split = True
            tokens = list(lex)
            break
        except ValueError:
            continue
    if tokens is None:
        return cwd
    i, n = 0, len(tokens)
    while i < n:
        if tokens[i] in ("cd", "pushd"):
            arg = tokens[i + 1] if i + 1 < n else None
            cwd = None if (arg is None or arg.startswith("-")) else resolve(cwd, arg)
            i += 2
            continue
        i += 1
    return cwd


def substitution_bodies(text, cwd=None):
    """[(body, directory)] for every CLOSED $( ) and ` ` span in text.

    Nesting is flattened: a body comes back with its own nested spans blanked
    to a single space and those nested bodies listed as further entries, so the
    caller hands each span to the analyzer exactly once and a nested one lands
    at the same recursion depth as one written bare. Blanking is what stops a
    nested body being analyzed twice, once on its own and once inside its
    parent. Entries are in OPENING order, parents before their children.

    A span whose delimiter never closes is not a span: it contributes no body
    and its text stays in the enclosing scope, so `a $( b` extracts nothing
    (pinned by "a surplus ( leaves the span unclosed and unread"). A `$( )`
    whose closing paren falls outside the enclosing span, and a backtick with
    no partner inside it, are unclosed by the same rule.

    One pass with a frame stack: every character is appended to exactly one
    scope's buffer, so the whole extraction is linear in the text rather than
    O(n*d) in the nesting depth, and no body is ever re-read (bd gqlc-hv81).

    `directory` is where the shell would run that span, per cd_target(); it is
    the caller's `cwd` when no `cd` precedes the span in its own scope.
    """
    closes = paren_matches(text)
    out = []
    frames = [{"buf": [], "end": len(text), "cwd": cwd, "slot": None}]
    i, n = 0, len(text)
    while i < n:
        frame = frames[-1]
        if i == frame["end"]:
            frames.pop()
            out[frame["slot"]] = ("".join(frame["buf"]), frame["cwd"])
            frames[-1]["buf"].append(" ")
            i += 1
            continue
        char = text[i]
        end = start = None
        if char == "$" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "(":
            close = closes.get(i + 1)
            if close is not None and close < frame["end"]:
                end, start = close, i + 2
        elif char == "`":
            close = text.find("`", i + 1)
            if close != -1 and close < frame["end"]:
                end, start = close, i + 1
        if end is None:
            frame["buf"].append(char)
            i += 1
            continue
        out.append(None)
        frames.append(
            {
                "buf": [],
                "end": end,
                "cwd": cd_target("".join(frame["buf"]), frame["cwd"]),
                "slot": len(out) - 1,
            }
        )
        i = start
    return out


def resolve(base, path):
    if path is None or is_op(path) or "$" in path or "`" in path or path.startswith("~"):
        return None
    if os.path.isabs(path):
        return path
    return os.path.join(base, path) if base else None


def shorthand_arg(opt, tokens, j):
    """(long flag name, value, tokens consumed) for a pflag shorthand cluster.

    (None, None, 1) when the token is a long flag, or when no letter in it is
    one of the value-taking shorthands. pflag walks a cluster left to right and
    the first letter that takes a value swallows the rest of the token — after
    one optional `=` — or the next token when the rest is empty. So `-rTEXT`,
    `-fr TEXT` and `-qr=TEXT` all reach `--reason`, and each of those three
    spellings verdicted allow-no-reason here before this existed.
    """
    if opt.startswith("--"):
        return None, None, 1
    for k in range(1, len(opt)):
        name = BD_SHORTHANDS_WITH_ARG.get(opt[k])
        if name is None:
            continue
        rest = opt[k + 1 :]
        if rest.startswith("="):
            return name, rest[1:], 1
        if rest:
            return name, rest, 1
        return name, (tokens[j + 1] if j + 1 < len(tokens) else None), 2
    return None, None, 1


def scan_bd(tokens, i, cwd):
    """Read one `bd ...` invocation starting at tokens[i].

    Returns (next_index, close_or_None, label_writes) where close is
    (effective_dir, saw_reason_flag, reason_text, reason_file) and label_writes
    is a list of (flag_or_"<positional>", label_value) this invocation would
    WRITE onto a bead — never one it merely filters by.
    """
    target, n = cwd, len(tokens)
    saw_reason, reason, reason_file, is_close = False, None, None, False
    # Positionals in order (the subcommand is the first) and every value handed
    # to a label-shaped flag, both collected unconditionally. Which of them is a
    # WRITE cannot be decided during the walk, because `-l` may be read before
    # the subcommand that gives it its meaning (`bd -C x -l foo create`).
    positionals, flagged = [], []
    j = i + 1
    while j < n:
        opt = tokens[j]
        step = redirect_skip(tokens, j)
        if step:
            j += step
            continue
        if is_op(opt):
            break
        name, val, inline = opt, None, False
        if opt.startswith("-") and "=" in opt:
            name, val = opt.split("=", 1)
            inline = True
        if name in BD_OPTS_WITH_ARG:
            if inline:
                j += 1
            else:
                val = tokens[j + 1] if j + 1 < n else None
                j += 2
            if name in ("-C", "--directory"):
                target = resolve(target, val)
            elif name in ("-r", "--reason"):
                saw_reason, reason = True, val
            elif name == "--reason-file":
                saw_reason, reason_file = True, val
            else:
                flagged.append((name, val))
            continue
        if opt.startswith("-"):
            name, val, step = shorthand_arg(opt, tokens, j)
            if name is not None:
                j += step
                if name == "--directory":
                    target = resolve(target, val)
                elif name == "--labels":
                    flagged.append((name, val))
                else:
                    saw_reason, reason = True, val
                continue
            j += 1
            continue
        if opt in BD_CLOSE_SUBCOMMANDS:
            is_close = True
        positionals.append(opt)
        j += 1
    close = (target, saw_reason, reason, reason_file) if is_close else None
    return j + 1, close, label_writes(positionals, flagged)


def label_writes(positionals, flagged):
    """Which of a `bd` invocation's values would actually be WRITTEN as labels.

    The subcommand decides. An invocation whose subcommand is not a label
    writer contributes nothing, which is what keeps `bd list -l <label>` — a
    filter spelled with the same letter — out of the guard entirely.
    """
    sub = positionals[0] if positionals else None
    writes = []
    for name, value in flagged:
        # `-l` reaches here already normalised to `--labels` by both paths.
        wanted = BD_LABEL_WRITE_OPTS.get(sub, ())
        if name in wanted or (name == "--labels" and "-l" in wanted):
            writes.extend((name, lab) for lab in split_labels(value))
    # `bd label add [issue-id...] [label]`. The label is the LAST positional,
    # after `label` and `add` themselves — so at least three are needed. With
    # only ids and no label, pflag would error; reading the final id as a label
    # here is inert, since a bead id is far short of the cap.
    if tuple(positionals[:2]) == BD_LABEL_POSITIONAL and len(positionals) >= 3:
        writes.append(("<positional>", positionals[-1]))
    return writes


def split_labels(value):
    """One flag value into the labels bd would store.

    cobra's `strings` flag splits on commas, so `-l a,b` is two labels and not
    one 3-character one — screening the value whole would refuse a legal pair
    for the sum of its parts. An empty element is dropped: it is not a label.
    """
    if value is None:
        return []
    return [part for part in value.split(",") if part]


def scan_tokens(tokens, cwd, wanted, closes=None, labels=None):
    targets = []
    i, n = 0, len(tokens)
    while i < n:
        tok = tokens[i]
        if tok == "bd" or tok.endswith("/bd"):
            i, close, writes = scan_bd(tokens, i, cwd)
            if close is not None and closes is not None:
                closes.append(close)
            if labels is not None:
                labels.extend(writes)
            continue
        if tok in ("cd", "pushd"):
            arg = tokens[i + 1] if i + 1 < n else None
            cwd = None if (arg is None or arg.startswith("-")) else resolve(cwd, arg)
            i += 2
            continue
        if tok == "git" or tok.endswith("/git"):
            target = cwd
            j = i + 1
            sub = None
            while j < n:
                opt = tokens[j]
                step = redirect_skip(tokens, j)
                if step:
                    j += step
                    continue
                if is_op(opt):
                    break
                if opt == "-C":
                    target = resolve(target, tokens[j + 1] if j + 1 < n else None)
                    j += 2
                elif opt in GIT_OPTS_WITH_ARG:
                    j += 2
                elif opt in ("--git-dir", "--work-tree"):
                    target = None
                    j += 2
                elif opt.startswith(("--git-dir=", "--work-tree=")):
                    target = None
                    j += 1
                elif opt.startswith("-"):
                    j += 1
                else:
                    sub = opt
                    break
            if sub in wanted and target is not None:
                targets.append((sub, target))
            i = j + 1
            continue
        i += 1
    return targets


def git_targets(text, cwd, wanted, depth=0, closes=None, labels=None):
    # -> [(subcommand, effective directory)], need_fallback
    if depth > 3:
        return [], False
    text, expanded = strip_heredocs(text)
    targets, need_fallback = [], False
    # The unquoted heredoc body contributes its substitutions and nothing else:
    # what substitution_bodies() extracts there is analyzed, the prose around
    # it is not, and the body text itself is never handed to the shlex call
    # below — what the extractor pulled out of it is, one recursion down.
    # Whether a matched substitution would in fact have run is not asked here —
    # an escaped one would not, and is analyzed anyway (header, under biases).
    # Same recursion level as a substitution written bare,
    # so the depth cap above sees no extra hop (measured: an unquoted heredoc
    # holding $(git commit) is found at depth 1, exactly where a bare
    # $(git commit) is found). substitution_bodies() flattens nesting for the
    # same reason, so the cap counts heredoc and substitution hops rather than
    # how deeply the writer nested their parens.
    bodies = substitution_bodies(text, cwd)
    bodies.extend(substitution_bodies(expanded, cwd))
    for body, body_cwd in bodies:
        sub_targets, sub_fallback = git_targets(
            body, body_cwd, wanted, depth + 1, closes, labels)
        targets.extend(sub_targets)
        need_fallback = need_fallback or sub_fallback
    try:
        lex = shlex.shlex(text, posix=True, punctuation_chars=True)
        lex.whitespace_split = True
        tokens = list(lex)
    except ValueError:
        return targets, True
    targets.extend(scan_tokens(tokens, cwd, wanted, closes, labels))
    return targets, need_fallback


def branch_of(directory):
    if not os.path.isdir(directory):
        return ""
    # Repo-discovery env (set e.g. inside git hooks) would override -C and
    # resolve the wrong repo; strip it so the target directory is authoritative.
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("GIT_")}
    for args in (("rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"), ("symbolic-ref", "--short", "HEAD")):
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run(
                ("git", "-C", directory) + args,
                capture_output=True,
                text=True,
                timeout=5,
                env=env,
            )
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            return ""
        name = proc.stdout.strip()
        if proc.returncode == 0 and name and name != "HEAD":
            return name
    return ""


def repo_root(directory):
    # git's own discovery rule: the first ancestor holding a .git entry — a
    # directory in a normal clone, a file in a linked worktree. Pure stat, no
    # subprocess, because this runs before every Bash call in every session.
    cur = os.path.realpath(directory)
    while True:
        if os.path.exists(os.path.join(cur, ".git")):
            return cur
        parent = os.path.dirname(cur)
        if parent == cur:
            return None
        cur = parent


def has_live_hook(directory):
    # git runs a hook only when it is an executable file, and it ships its
    # templates suffixed .sample so it will skip them. Restating that rule is
    # what makes this "points at hooks that will run" rather than "points
    # somewhere": an existence or non-emptiness test is satisfied by a
    # directory of executable *.sample files, which is what a default
    # .git/hooks holds and what both recorded drifts pointed at (bd gqlc-5fm,
    # gqlc-hd68). Those two are caught one step earlier, by the value
    # comparison; this covers the residue the value cannot see — hooksPath
    # correct, .githooks/ present, and nothing in it that git would execute.
    try:
        with os.scandir(directory) as entries:
            for entry in entries:
                if entry.name.endswith(".sample"):
                    continue
                if entry.is_file() and os.access(entry.path, os.X_OK):
                    return True
    except OSError:
        return False
    return False


_DRIFT_CACHE = {}


def hooks_drift(directory):
    """Reason string when .githooks/ is not what git will run, else None.

    Lives here rather than in a git hook because a deactivated git hook does
    not run and so cannot report its own deactivation, and the drift
    deactivates all of .githooks/ at once, repo-wide — linked worktrees share
    one .git/config. This file is wired through .claude/settings.json, so
    core.hooksPath does not govern whether it runs. What that buys is latency,
    and in two states coverage as well. Latency: `just check-hooks` runs when
    someone runs it, in practice as a dependency of `just test` / `just
    doctor`, and the window between the drifting write and the next such run
    is what went unobserved for 30+ minutes across five sessions (bd
    gqlc-nzwa). Coverage: check-hooks compares the configured value and
    nothing else, so on the two states has_live_hook() below adds — value
    correct, .githooks/ present, holding only *.sample files or a hook left
    non-executable — check-hooks exits 0 and `just doctor` prints "ok" while
    this refuses (measured; both states are fixtures in the test suite,
    DEAD_REPO and NOEXEC_REPO). The latency half applies only to Bash tool
    calls inside a Claude Code session; nothing here runs for a plain
    terminal, an editor's git integration or a non-Bash tool, and
    `just check-hooks` is what those have.

    Silent — never a reason — for a cwd outside any repo and for repos that
    ship no .githooks/. This runs ahead of every Bash call in every session,
    including the ones needed to repair the drift, so anything it cannot
    positively identify as this repo's broken state has to fail open.
    """
    root = repo_root(directory)
    if root is None:
        return None
    if root in _DRIFT_CACHE:
        return _DRIFT_CACHE[root]
    reason = None
    hooks = os.path.join(root, HOOKS_DIRNAME)
    if os.path.isdir(hooks):
        # Two kinds of GIT_* env, and they need opposite treatment. DISCOVERY
        # vars (GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE, GIT_COMMON_DIR, GIT_INDEX_FILE, ...)
        # would redirect -C at whichever repo set them, so they go. VALUE vars
        # — GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS and the GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY_n/VALUE_n triple,
        # which are how `git -c core.hooksPath=X ...` reaches its descendants —
        # are what git itself will read when it decides whether to run a hook,
        # so stripping them makes this report a value git will not use.
        #
        # Dropping every GIT_* did exactly that. Measured (bd gqlc-o13d,
        # 2026-08-22): with .git/config saying '.githooks' and
        # GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS="'core.hooksPath=/dev/null'" exported, a real
        # `git commit` ran NO hook and landed, while this detector stayed
        # SILENT. That is the fail-open direction on the one state it exists to
        # catch, and it needs no writer — which is why no writer was ever found
        # for the /dev/null sightings this bead was filed on.
        #
        # GIT_CONFIG (no trailing underscore, deprecated) is deliberately NOT
        # kept: git honours it for `git config` and not for the config it reads
        # elsewhere, so honouring it here would report a file the hook runner
        # never consults.
        env = {
            k: v
            for k, v in os.environ.items()
            if not k.startswith("GIT_") or k.startswith("GIT_CONFIG_")
        }
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run(
                ("git", "-C", root, "config", "--show-origin", "--get", "core.hooksPath"),
                capture_output=True,
                text=True,
                timeout=5,
                env=env,
            )
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            proc = None
        if proc is not None:
            # rc 1 is "not set", which is drift: git then runs $GIT_DIR/hooks.
            raw = proc.stdout.rstrip("\n") if proc.returncode == 0 else ""
            # --show-origin prints "<origin>\t<value>". partition, not split,
            # because the unset case leaves raw empty and has to yield an empty
            # origin without a separate guard for it.
            origin, _, value = raw.partition("\t")
            value = value.strip()
            if value != HOOKS_DIRNAME:
                # The two drift shapes need opposite repairs, and the VALUE does
                # not tell them apart — /dev/null and an absolute path each show
                # up under both origins. 'command line:' is what git reports for
                # GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS and the COUNT/KEY/VALUE triple as well as
                # for a literal -c, so it means "this arrived through the
                # environment": .git/config is already correct and `just init`
                # rewrites a file that was never wrong, reports success, and
                # changes nothing. Naming the origin is what lets a SINGLE firing
                # settle which shape it is, which is the question still open on
                # bd gqlc-o13d. No claim here about which shape the sightings on
                # that bead were — that is what this is meant to find out.
                if origin == "command line:":
                    repair = (
                        "this came from the ENVIRONMENT (GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS, or the "
                        "GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY_n/VALUE_n triple, as exported by a `git -c "
                        "core.hooksPath=...` somewhere up the process tree), not from "
                        "%s/.git/config, which is unchanged. `just init` will not help. "
                        "Repair: clear it in the process that exported it" % root
                    )
                else:
                    repair = "%s. Repair: just init" % (
                        "from %s" % origin if origin else "origin unreported by git"
                    )
                # No list of hook names here, and no count of them (bd
                # gqlc-tr3c). The string this replaces asserted that
                # "commit-msg, pre-commit, pre-push and post-merge are all
                # dead", which is a four-way universal stated at the moment of
                # refusal — and false in exactly the fail-closed case below it,
                # where the configured path is an absolute spelling of a
                # directory whose hooks are live and firing. A reader told a
                # named hook is dead when it is running debugs the wrong thing,
                # and this string is what a human sees while being refused. A
                # shorter list would be the same defect with a different number,
                # so what is stated is the negative that holds under every
                # value: git is not reading the repo's own hooks directory, and
                # this hook does not open the configured path to see what is in
                # it.
                reason = (
                    "local git hooks are inactive: core.hooksPath in %s is %s, not '%s', "
                    "so git is not running the hooks this repository ships and what a "
                    "gated command is screened by is whatever the configured path holds "
                    "— not read here. %s"
                    % (
                        root,
                        "'%s'" % value if value else "<unset>",
                        HOOKS_DIRNAME,
                        repair,
                    )
                )
            elif not has_live_hook(hooks):
                reason = (
                    "local git hooks are inactive: core.hooksPath in %s is '%s', but "
                    "%s holds no executable hook (git ignores *.sample). "
                    "Repair: restore the hook scripts, then just init"
                    % (root, HOOKS_DIRNAME, hooks)
                )
    _DRIFT_CACHE[root] = reason
    return reason


_EMITTED = False


def emit(payload):
    global _EMITTED
    _EMITTED = True
    print(json.dumps(payload))


def git_env():
    # Repo-discovery env (set e.g. inside git hooks) would override -C and
    # resolve the wrong repo; strip it so the target directory is authoritative.
    return {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("GIT_")}


def object_types(directory, shas):
    """Map each sha to its git object type, "" when absent.

    Returns None when git could not answer at all — a state kept distinct from
    "absent" so an unrunnable probe is never read as evidence of anything.
    `git cat-file --batch-check` emits exactly one line per input line, in
    order, and exits 0 even when every object is missing; the mapping is
    therefore positional, because a found object's line carries the FULL oid
    rather than the abbreviation that was asked for.
    """
    if not directory or not os.path.isdir(directory):
        return None
    revs = [s for s in shas if s]  # never hand git an empty rev
    if not revs:
        return None
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ("git", "-C", directory, "cat-file", "--batch-check"),
            input="".join(s + "\n" for s in revs),
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            timeout=10,
            env=git_env(),
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    lines = proc.stdout.splitlines()
    if proc.returncode != 0 or len(lines) != len(revs):
        return None
    types = {}
    for rev, line in zip(revs, lines):
        fields = line.split()
        types[rev] = fields[1] if len(fields) >= 2 and fields[1] != "missing" else ""
    return types


def remote_refs_containing(directory, sha):
    """(ok, refs) for `git branch -r --contains <sha>`.

    ok is False when the probe could not answer, which is NOT the same value as
    an empty ref list.

    The `not sha` clause is defence in depth, not a live guard: removing it is
    behaviour-preserving here, because the argv is a list and git answers an
    empty rev with `error: malformed object name` and rc 129, which this reads
    as "could not tell" anyway. It is here because that is only true of a list
    argv. `git branch -r --contains $SHA` in a shell, with SHA unset, passes NO
    argument, and git then defaults to HEAD and prints the remote refs
    containing it — measured — so the same probe rewritten as a shell string
    would report the unpushed close this hook exists to refuse as reachable.
    """
    if not sha or not directory or not os.path.isdir(directory):
        return False, []
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ("git", "-C", directory, "branch", "-r", "--contains", sha),
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            timeout=10,
            env=git_env(),
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return False, []
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return False, []
    return True, [ln.strip() for ln in proc.stdout.splitlines() if ln.strip()]


def reason_text(close):
    """(text, unreadable_explanation). Exactly one of the two is None."""
    directory, _, reason, reason_file = close
    if reason_file is not None:
        if reason_file == "-" or UNEXPANDED_RE.search(reason_file):
            return None, "the reason is read from stdin or a path this hook cannot resolve"
        # bd opens a relative path from the directory IT runs in, which the
        # tuple carries as element 0; this hook's own cwd is unrelated to it.
        # Under a name collision the two are different files, so reading the
        # hook's copy answers about a file nobody closed against (bd gqlc-d26r).
        path = resolve(directory, reason_file)
        if path is None:
            return None, (
                "--reason-file %s is not a path this hook can resolve from the directory "
                "bd will run in" % reason_file
            )
        try:
            with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
                return handle.read(), None
        except OSError as exc:
            return None, "--reason-file %s could not be read (%s)" % (path, exc.strerror)
    if reason is None:
        return None, "the reason flag carries no value on this command line"
    if UNEXPANDED_RE.search(reason):
        return None, "the reason is built by the shell, so its text is not visible here"
    return reason, None


def close_verdict(close):
    """(verdict_name, deny_message_or_None) for one `bd close` invocation.

    Every outcome is a named verdict — including the three that allow
    (allow-no-reason, allow-no-sha, allow-reachable) — so a reason that cites
    nothing is a decision on the record rather than a branch nobody took.
    """
    directory, saw_reason, _, _ = close
    if not saw_reason:
        return "allow-no-reason", None
    text, unreadable = reason_text(close)
    if text is None:
        return "deny-unreadable-reason", (
            "`bd close` was given a reason this hook cannot read, so it cannot check "
            "whether that reason cites an unpushed commit: %s. Pass the reason as a "
            "literal -r/--reason string, or as a --reason-file with a literal path."
            % unreadable
        )
    anchored = {s for s in CITED_SHA_RE.findall(text) if not DECIMAL_TOKEN_RE.match(s)}
    loose = set(SHA_RE.findall(text)) - anchored
    if not anchored and not loose:
        return "allow-no-sha", None
    unverifiable = (
        "`bd close` cites commit sha(s) %s, and this hook could not %%s, so it cannot "
        "check whether any remote ref contains them. Run the close from a literal "
        "directory, or pass `bd -C <literal path>`." % ", ".join(sorted(anchored))
    )
    # When the repo cannot be reached, an ANCHORED sha is still a citation and is
    # refused; a loose hex token is not, because promoting one to a citation is
    # exactly the question that needed the repo. Denying on loose tokens alone
    # would refuse `-r "the docs were defaced"` from a directory this hook
    # cannot resolve, which is prose, not a close against unpushed work.
    if not directory or not os.path.isdir(directory):
        if not anchored:
            return "allow-no-sha", None
        return "deny-unverifiable-repo", unverifiable % "resolve the repository the command runs in"
    types = object_types(directory, sorted(anchored | loose))
    if types is None:
        if not anchored:
            return "allow-no-sha", None
        return "deny-unverifiable-repo", unverifiable % "get an answer from `git cat-file --batch-check`"
    # A loose token becomes a citation only once git says it names a commit here.
    citations = sorted(anchored | {s for s in loose if types.get(s) == "commit"})
    if not citations:
        return "allow-no-sha", None
    for sha in citations:
        if types.get(sha, "") != "commit":
            return "deny-absent-object", (
                "`bd close` cites %s, which names no commit in %s (`git cat-file -t %s` "
                "finds nothing there). A close cannot record work against a commit this "
                "repository has never seen." % (sha, directory, sha)
            )
        ok, refs = remote_refs_containing(directory, sha)
        if not ok:
            return "deny-unverifiable-repo", (
                "`bd close` cites %s and `git branch -r --contains %s` in %s gave no "
                "answer, so reachability could not be checked." % (sha, sha, directory)
            )
        if not refs:
            return "deny-unpushed-sha", (
                "`bd close` cites %s, which no remote-tracking ref contains: "
                "`git branch -r --contains %s` in %s printed nothing (the ref set "
                "checked is refs/remotes only — local branches and remote tags do not "
                "count). That commit lives only in this checkout, so a rebase discards "
                "it while the close stands, recording work that never landed "
                "(bd gqlc-90vt). Push the branch, then close citing a sha a remote ref "
                "contains." % (sha, sha, directory)
            )
    return "allow-reachable", None


# Resolved beside this hook first, because all of its callers invoke it by path,
# and off the work tree only as a fallback — the same order bd-gh-sync uses to
# find the same file.
LABEL_GATE_PATHS = (
    os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)),
                 "..", ".github", "scripts", "check-label-lengths.py"),
)


@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=1)
def label_rule():
    """(cap, prefix, remedy_fn) from the module that owns them, or None.

    None means the cap could not be read, which is NOT the same as "no label is
    over it" — see label_verdict, which reports the difference rather than
    defaulting to a number. Cached because this hook runs ahead of every Bash
    call and the import reads and compiles a file.
    """
    paths = list(LABEL_GATE_PATHS)
    root = repo_root(os.getcwd())
    if root:
        paths.append(os.path.join(root, ".github", "scripts", "check-label-lengths.py"))
    for path in paths:
        if not os.path.isfile(path):
            continue
        try:
            # Importing compiles, and python caches that compilation next to the
            # source — a hook writing __pycache__/ into .github/scripts/, which
            # is not gitignored and would leave an untracked directory behind in
            # every session. bd-gh-sync hit this on its first run of this path.
            saved, sys.dont_write_bytecode = sys.dont_write_bytecode, True
            try:
                spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("_label_gate", path)
                if spec is None or spec.loader is None:
                    continue
                mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
                spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
            finally:
                sys.dont_write_bytecode = saved
            return int(mod.MAX_LABEL), str(mod.PREFIX), mod.remedy
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - every failure here has one meaning
            continue
    return None


def label_verdict(write):
    """(verdict name, message or None) for one label a `bd` command would write.

    Five verdicts — no-label-write, allow-unexpanded-label, warn-cap-unreadable,
    allow-label-fits, deny-label-over-cap — of which only the last refuses.
    Named rather than reduced to a boolean because "no label was written" and
    "the arm stopped finding labels" are the same silence otherwise.
    """
    flag, label = write
    if label is None:
        return "no-label-write", None
    # A value the shell has not expanded has no length to measure — `$(...)`
    # could resolve to anything. Allowed, and named so the gap is legible: the
    # label lands unscreened, and WHAT catches it then depends on the bead. One
    # with no mirror yet meets bd-gh-sync's push-time refusal; one that already
    # has a mirror is skipped by that screen entirely, so nothing looks at the
    # label until the CI gate. Refusing on the unexpanded text's own length
    # would refuse a command whose real label fits.
    if UNEXPANDED_RE.search(label):
        return "allow-unexpanded-label", None
    rule = label_rule()
    if rule is None:
        return "warn-cap-unreadable", (
            "A label GitHub can hold could not be checked: the cap lives in "
            ".github/scripts/check-label-lengths.py and that file could not be read, "
            "so this label was NOT screened. If this bead has no mirror yet, a label "
            "over the cap makes GitHub refuse the whole issue and leaves it without "
            "one; if it is already mirrored, the label never reaches GitHub at all "
            "and CI is the only thing that will object (bd gqlc-uy7j)."
        )
    cap, _prefix, remedy = rule
    if len(label) <= cap:
        return "allow-label-fits", None
    # `bd -l` would be a command nobody typed: the subcommand is not carried
    # this far, so name the flag as a flag. The positional path has no flag to
    # name, which is why it names its subcommand instead.
    where = "`%s` value" % flag if flag != "<positional>" else "`bd label add`"
    message = (
        "That %s would create a label GitHub cannot hold: %r is %d characters and "
        "the cap is %d. On a bead with no mirror yet, GitHub refuses the WHOLE issue "
        "over one bad label, so the bead would be left without one and you would not "
        "find out here: the complaint surfaces at `git push`, in whoever's session "
        "pushes next. On a bead that is ALREADY mirrored, bd-gh-sync skips it, so the "
        "label reaches nobody and nothing objects until CI (bd gqlc-uy7j)."
        % (where, label, len(label), cap)
    )
    advice = remedy(label)
    if advice:
        message += " " + advice
    return "deny-label-over-cap", message


def deny(reason):
    print("ERROR: %s" % reason, file=sys.stderr)
    emit(
        {
            "hookSpecificOutput": {
                "hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
                "permissionDecision": "deny",
                "permissionDecisionReason": reason,
            }
        }
    )


def warn(reason):
    # Non-blocking on purpose, and deliberately NOT permissionDecision:"allow"
    # — that would waive the permission prompt for every command it touched.
    # systemMessage reaches the user, additionalContext reaches the model.
    print("WARN: %s" % reason, file=sys.stderr)
    emit(
        {
            "systemMessage": reason,
            "hookSpecificOutput": {
                "hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
                "additionalContext": reason,
            },
        }
    )


def main():
    data = json.load(sys.stdin)
    command = (data.get("tool_input") or {}).get("command")
    if not isinstance(command, str) or not command.strip():
        return
    cwd = os.getcwd()
    closes = []
    labels = []
    targets, need_fallback = git_targets(
        command, cwd, HOOK_GATED, closes=closes, labels=labels)
    if need_fallback and FALLBACK_RE.search(command):
        targets.append(("commit", cwd))
    for sub, directory in targets:
        if sub != "commit":
            continue
        branch = branch_of(directory)
        if branch in PROTECTED:
            deny(
                "Direct commits to %s are blocked (target: %s). "
                "Create a feature branch with: git switch -c <branch>" % (branch, directory)
            )
            return

    for close in closes:
        # An exception escaping this loop reaches __main__, which WARNS and
        # allows — deliberate there, so a bug in this hook cannot block its own
        # repair. For a close that is the defect itself: the command runs, the
        # ledger records work that may never have landed, and the warning
        # arrives after the damage. Caught here and turned into a refusal
        # instead, which is the one arm where "could not check" must not mean
        # "let it through". An exception thrown EARLIER, while tokenizing,
        # still fails open: that is the master guard's pre-existing bias and
        # changing it would change that guard's contract, not this one's.
        try:
            _, message = close_verdict(close)
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - deliberate: an exception denies
            message = (
                "`bd close` could not be checked for an unpushed sha citation (%s: %s), "
                "so it is refused rather than allowed through unverified (bd gqlc-90vt)."
                % (type(exc).__name__, exc)
            )
        if message is not None:
            deny(message)
            return

    # The label arm. Unlike the close arm, an exception here is NOT turned into
    # a refusal: the damage this prevents is recoverable in one command by the
    # person standing at the keyboard (edit the label and retype), while a bug
    # in the arm that denied would block `bd create` in every concurrent session
    # at once, including whichever one has to fix it. So it falls through to the
    # top-level handler, which warns and allows.
    for write in labels:
        verdict, message = label_verdict(write)
        if message is None:
            continue
        if verdict == "deny-label-over-cap":
            deny(message)
            return
        warn(message)
        return

    # A dead hook tree is repo state, not a property of this command, so the
    # refusal is scoped to the commands the dead hooks would have gated. Every
    # other command — `just init` included — still runs, which is what keeps a
    # broken repo config from wedging the session that has to repair it.
    for sub, directory in targets:
        reason = hooks_drift(directory)
        if reason is not None:
            deny("git %s refused — %s" % (sub, reason))
            return
    # Nothing gated in this command: still say so, on the first Bash call after
    # the drift rather than on the next `just test`.
    reason = hooks_drift(cwd)
    if reason is not None:
        warn(reason)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        main()
    except Exception as exc:
        # An internal error must not read as "nothing to report". This hook
        # exists to refuse when the git hooks are dead, so exiting silently on
        # its own bug is the failure it was written to catch. Warn rather than
        # deny: it runs ahead of every Bash call in every concurrent session,
        # so a bug that denied would wedge the session that has to repair it,
        # including `just init`. Suppressed once a decision has been emitted —
        # a second JSON object on stdout would not parse, and the decision
        # already reached the caller.
        if not _EMITTED:
            try:
                warn(
                    "claude-pre-bash failed internally (%s: %s) — the "
                    "master-branch and core.hooksPath checks did NOT run for "
                    "this command. Verify the hooks with: just check-hooks"
                    % (type(exc).__name__, exc)
                )
            except Exception:
                pass
    sys.exit(0)
